+#### options.capabilities
+
+- Type: `boolean | CapabilitiesOptions`
+- Default: `true`
+
+Links each production client build against the smallest Solid runtime its
+module graph can use, through Solid's capability linker (`solidCapabilities`
+from `@solidjs/compiler/capabilities`). The linker proves the graph from
+every application module's compiled output and the library packages'
+capability manifests (`<package>/capabilities.json`), then:
+
+- resolves `@solidjs/signals` to its async-free entry when the whole graph is
+  proven async-free;
+- compiles out every runtime feature switch the graph is proven not to use
+  (`OPTIMISTIC`, `VERDICTS`, `STORES`, `SNAPSHOTS`, `ITERABLE`,
+  `COMPILED_SEAMS`).
+
+Anything unproven (a library without a manifest, say) keeps the full runtime:
+the linker never guesses. It runs in `vite build` only — the dev server and
+vitest keep the full runtime — and in client environments only unless
+`server: true`. With an `@solidjs/compiler` that predates the linker, builds
+are unchanged (the build log says so once). A config that adds
+`solidCapabilities()` itself keeps its own instance.
+
+`false` opts out. An object tunes the linker:
+
+| Option          | Default | Meaning                                                                              |
+| --------------- | ------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |
+| `features`      | `true`  | Slice the runtime's feature switches.                                                |
+| `compiledFacts` | `true`  | Prove the switches from compiled output (`false`: from authored imports).            |
+| `compiledSeams` | `true`  | Whether the build's compiler passes may emit compiled seams.                         |
+| `typedSummary`  | —       | `solid-tsc --capabilities` output (path relative to the root, or the parsed object). |
+| `report`        | —       | Write the linker's decision (what was switched off, and why not) to this path.       |
+| `entries`       | —       | Entry modules when the build input does not name them (builds normally need none).   |
+| `server`        | `false` | Also link server environments (each environment is proven over its own graph).       |
+
+```ts
+import { defineConfig } from 'vite';
+import solidPlugin from '@solidjs/vite-plugin';
+
+export default defineConfig({
+  plugins: [solidPlugin({ capabilities: { report: 'capabilities-report.json' } })],
+});
+```
+
